---
name: type-ascription-tuple
description: "case _: TupleN matches a tuple of that arity and nothing else, on every lane"
backends: [int, js, jvm, v2]
---

# `case _: TupleN`

`BUGS.md` `type-ascription-tuple-and-set-arms-missing`. Sibling of `type-ascription-unit`: the
same two type-test tables had no arm for tuples, so the pattern fell through to the wildcard on
INT and JS while the native lane matched it — native represents a tuple as `DataV("Tuple2", …)`,
so its ordinary nominal test already worked.

```scalascript
def kind(x: Any): String = x match
  case _: Tuple2[?, ?] => "pair"
  case _: Tuple3[?, ?, ?] => "triple"
  case _ => "other"

println(kind((1, 2)))
println(kind((1, 2, 3)))
println(kind(List(1, 2)))
println(kind("ab"))
```

The type parameters are not decoration. Bare `case _: Tuple2` is a **compile error** in Scala 3 —
`Missing type parameter for [T1, T2] =>> (T1, T2)` — so the JVM lane, which is the only lane that
runs real Scala and is therefore the oracle here, cannot even build it. The wildcard spelling is
what all four lanes accept; INT and JS see the same `Tuple2` name either way, since the pattern
arrives as a `Type.Apply` whose head is what the test reads.

Arity is the part worth pinning down: `case _: Tuple2[?, ?]` must be **false** for a 3-tuple, so
the test cannot be a prefix match on the name. The line above that prints `triple` for a 3-tuple is
what distinguishes a correct arm from one that matches any tuple at all.

A two-element `List` must not match either. That is not hypothetical on the JS lane, where a tuple
*is* a JS array — it carries an `_isTuple` marker precisely so the two can be told apart, and the
`List(1, 2)` line above is what proves the marker is being consulted.

```scalascript
val xs = List((1, 2), (1, 2, 3), List(4, 5), 7)
println(xs.map(x => kind(x)).mkString(","))
```
